{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1735 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "4ab850f87f7f77a4f6675087c18ee4e38c872b663089cc67618d69bb6dffbd4b" :edb/db-before-t 1734 :edb/db-after-t 1735 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535047 50 #inst "2026-10-08T15:35:18.406Z" 13194139535047 true] [17592186156928 1145 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x), whose ordinary integral exists over the interval, the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+A & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe displayed example integrands are continuous on their intervals. If an integrand has an internal singularity, first check the convergence of every one-sided improper integral; symmetry cannot cancel independently divergent integrals. A Cauchy principal value requires an explicit principal-value convention." 13194139535047 true] [17592186156928 1145 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ abou